# Add lint + typecheck to the toolchain

The project has no ruff/mypy/pyright setup and no C++ static analysis;
the 2026-08-23 review found dead code, duplicate definitions and
type leaks that linters would have flagged.

Fix: add ruff (generator + huggorm), mypy or pyright for
the non-generated modules (generated code excluded), and clang-tidy or
at least -Wall fixes pass for the mock. Wire into a `nix run` check
and CI-able script.

## Update 2026-08-25

Structural gates now exist where a linter cannot reach, all failing
the build:

- emitted modules must import exactly the names they use (this is what
  caught the sync Derivation arriving unused in every wrapper);
- emitted modules and classes must carry a real docstring;
- no module above the bindings may name a manifest class in a string
  literal (test_remote);
- the emitter-runtime symbol contract (011);
- the wire contract: policy, fields, helpers and threading must agree.

The 2026-08-25 review still found by hand what a linter would have
flagged in a second: a dead _PRIMITIVES table in pxd.py, a dead `hide`
parameter, a dead `import asyncio` inside a function, unused imports
in custom.py. That is the argument for actually adding ruff.

Remaining, unchanged: ruff over generator + huggorm
(generated package excluded - the smoke gates cover it), mypy or
pyright over the hand-written modules, and a -Wall/clang-tidy pass
over the mock. The generated wrappers are now typed honestly enough
for mypy to be worth pointing at consumers (see 017).

## Update 2026-08-25 (second)

017 pointed mypy --strict at the emitted package for the first time,
which produced a concrete inventory rather than an intention. A
consumer typed against the generated protocols is CLEAN. The package
itself is not, and the errors group cleanly:

    _runtime.py       38   untyped defs, untyped calls, bare type-args
    rpc.py            19   Any returns from client.invoke; untyped client
    async_*.py        21   Any returns from _runner.call; _runner unknown
                           on the abstract base; untyped __init__
    free_functions.py  4   Any returns; bare `dict` from gc_stats
    bindings           2   no stubs for the .so (see 027)

Most of it has one cause: `_runtime.py` is hand-written and carries no
annotations, so every call into it returns Any and every emitted
method that forwards to it reports no-any-return. Annotating the
runtime is therefore the first move, not the last - it collapses three
of those five rows.

Two follow from it: the emitted classes should declare `_runner`
(mypy cannot see it on the abstract base, which never assigns it), and
the rpc classes should type their `client` parameter - a small
Protocol declaring invoke and release, which keeps the dependency
pointing the right way.

027 covers the bindings row and is independent of this task.

## Done 2026-08-25

Both tools are in, both gate every build, and everything they cover is
clean.

### zuban rather than mypy

Carl asked for zuban to be explored, and it wins on the only axis that
was in question. It is mypy-compatible in the strict sense: the same
flags, and byte-identical diagnostics on every comparison run here -
the emitted package, a correct consumer, and a file of five deliberate
mistakes. It is about thirty times faster:

    mypy 2.1.0   2.15s     zuban 0.9.1   0.06s     (same 11 files, same 2 errors)

That is the difference between a check you run on every build and one
you run when you remember.

One trap: passing a glob of *.py makes zuban treat each file as a
standalone module and invent import errors. Pass the DIRECTORY. mypy
infers the package from __init__.py either way.

### What it found

The generated package went 95 -> 0 and the hand-written layer 132 -> 0,
but the count is not the point. These are the findings that were not
annotations:

- the reaper's shutdown task was fire-and-forget, and asyncio holds
  only a weak reference to a running task, so it could be collected
  before it ran. That is the path that shuts an affine runner down, and
  therefore also where its thread leaves the collector's list (ruff
  RUF006);
- run_remote called Task.exception() twice, once to test and once to
  raise, and exception() raises rather than returns if the task was
  cancelled between the two;
- HandleTable.on_drop was typed None by being assigned None, so the
  server installing a callback on it was an error;
- json.loads(e.message) - grpclib's GRPCError.message is Optional;
- BaseRunner never declared aclose(), which every subclass has and
  every emitted wrapper calls;
- two names meaning two things in one function, in generate.py and in
  the smoke test;
- a dead import, a dead local, and a closure written in a loop body;
- two GRPCError raises inside except clauses with no cause attached.

### The gates

    codegen                 the generator's own source
    huggorm-generated  the emitted package and its stubs
    huggorm     the hand-written layer and both suites

plus `nix run --file . check` for the whole tree without a rebuild,
which is slightly broader - the per-package gates leave the setup.py
files uncovered, and it caught one of those immediately.

Verified by perturbation: removing a single parameter annotation fails
the build naming the file and line.

### Decisions taken

Line length 100, not ruff's 88. The prose here wraps near 72 and the
code sits under 100; 88 reported thirty-odd lines that were fine, which
is how a rule gets ignored.

bandit and blind-except are not selected. Three places catch broadly on
purpose, each with a comment saying why. A rule silenced everywhere it
fires measures nothing.

The generated package gets no ruff. The smoke gates are stricter about
what matters there and say things a linter cannot.

protobuf ships no stubs for its own generated descriptor module, so
five call sites carry a targeted ignore naming the reason rather than
the error code being switched off across a package.

### Not done

clang-tidy over the mock. It belongs to whoever owns that code, which
stops being us when the mock is replaced by real Nix (tasks/015).

Correction, 2026-08-25: -Wall was never open. meson.build has carried
`warning_level=2` since the mock was written, which is -Wall -Wextra,
and the mock has always compiled clean under it. This file said
otherwise and was wrong.
